WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:06.169
Let's look at adding artificial lights or man-made lights to accommodate night scenarios.

2
00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:11.789
For that, you need to open the file named To-Lighting that's been provided to you.

3
00:00:12.189 --> 00:00:18.489
Before you move on, create a new active container in the scene graph named Lights to keep things tidy.

4
00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:23.219
All the lights you create in this exercise will be part of that container.

5
00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:29.039
By default, when you start a project, the daytime lighting scenario is provided to you.

6
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:33.600
To set your lighting at night, you first need to change the time of day.

7
00:00:34.869 --> 00:00:41.579
This can be done in the Eye menu in the viewport or in the Ambience panel.

8
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:51.399
Interestingly, if you go beyond a certain hour of the day, after the sun sets, some aspects of your scene react to that change.

9
00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:56.579
A good example is the headlights and taillights on animated vehicles.

10
00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:02.450
Change the time of day going from day to night to see the cars light up.

11
00:01:07.459 --> 00:01:13.159
However, you do need to place a few active lights in and around the project, which appears rather dark at night.

12
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.000
Let's see what's available.

13
00:01:16.879 --> 00:01:19.579
Go to the Assets library under Lights category.

14
00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:25.340
You'll see many entries, but most are samples of lights with an IES profile.

15
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.739
Essentially, all of those are spotlights.

16
00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.340
There are really four options to choose from.

17
00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.230
An Omni or Point light that emanates in all directions.

18
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.900
You can control the intensity.

19
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.599
Its attenuation or how far it spreads.

20
00:01:46.579 --> 00:01:47.780
The color temperature.

21
00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.090
And whether it casts shadows, among other options.

22
00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:56.859
A spotlight on the other hand is more directional.

23
00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:00.890
It opens up in a cone so that you can control the beam.

24
00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:06.280
But otherwise, has roughly the same parameters as an Omni light.

25
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.300
Try an IES light such as IES 03.

26
00:02:18.759 --> 00:02:21.060
You'll notice it effectively is a spotlight.

27
00:02:22.139 --> 00:02:32.620
It has the same options, except for an IES profile defined in the Lights miscellaneous section.

28
00:02:33.669 --> 00:02:38.870
That profile ensures the light spreads in a particular way, the way the manufacturer intended.

29
00:02:39.979 --> 00:02:45.280
Incidentally, if you need to select a light in the scene, you can do so in the scene graph.

30
00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:53.020
If you wish to select it in the viewport, you may or may not see an icon associated with the light.

31
00:02:53.719 --> 00:02:57.520
If you do not see the icons, press G to display them.

32
00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.020
The G key acts as a toggle.

33
00:03:02.699 --> 00:03:14.280
You also have a linear light, or a neon light as it's called, where you can control intensity, length and color, but not attenuation.

34
00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:40.439
Finally, there's also an Area light, and as you define parameters such as length, width and attenuation, you get a light that emanates in a box-like pattern.

35
00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.439
This can be used to help with global illumination in difficult spots.

36
00:03:55.229 --> 00:04:00.030
As a side note, night lighting is where Lumen shines, no pun intended.

37
00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:05.310
You can better appreciate the difference between Lumen and Standard modes in night scenes.

38
00:04:11.729 --> 00:04:17.329
In addition to their regular parameters, all light types can be set to activate only at nighttime.

39
00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:24.860
Currently, if you switch back to Day mode, the lights are still active.

40
00:04:27.019 --> 00:04:33.519
But if you select one of them and go to the Miscellaneous section, you can enable the Dusk to Dawn option.

41
00:04:35.009 --> 00:04:37.709
You can do the same with every light you have placed in the scene.

42
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.879
If you try it now, you'll notice that the lights only activate around 7.30 or 8pm.

43
00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:02.060
In addition to standalone lights, there are some assets in the library that have embedded lights in them.

44
00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:11.680
For example, go to Objects -> City -> Street Lights and drag in a street lamp. I'll use this one called Streamlight.

45
00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:20.860
Note in the scene graph that this asset is made of both geometry and a spotlight that you can control and adjust to your liking.

46
00:05:24.779 --> 00:05:27.980
However, not all assets have associated lights.

47
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:39.759
For example, try this bollard named Galvanic. It's just a simple mesh at this time, but you can help change it a bit.

48
00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.899
Try adding a self-illuminated material to the white covers.

49
00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:53.550
If you're using Lumen, the emissive material helps a little bit with the lighting.

50
00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:59.449
You can also add a full-fledged omni light to help illuminate the area around the bollard.

51
00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:15.740
You can then parent the light to the bollard and send the bollard to the user library.

52
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.500
You now have your own assets ready and available.

53
00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:33.660
If you make instances of any light in the scene, you'll be able to change the parameters of one light to update all duplicates.

54
00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.959
Keep on experimenting by creating your own light fixtures.

55
00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:42.560
Next, we'll discuss media output.
